==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|welefare}}, probably from the {{der|en|ang|-}} phrase {{m|ang|wel faran|t=to fare well, get along successfully, prosper}} (cognate with {{cog|dum|welvare|t=welfare}}, {{cog|gml|wolvare|t=welfare}}, {{cog|gmh|wolvar}}, {{m|gmh|wolfar|t=welfare}}). Equivalent to {{af|en|well|fare}}. Compare also {{cog|fy|wolfeart}}, {{cog|nl|welvaart}}, {{cog|de|Wohlfahrt}}, {{cog|non|velferð}} (whence {{cog|sv|välfärd|t=welfare}}). 

The first recorded use in the sense of "social concern for the well-being of children, the unemployed, etc." is from 1904 and in the sense of "organized effort to provide for maintenance of members of a group" from 1918.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

